/** Full translations siman 091 — YD001 editorial pass */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `To rinse. Bach wrote specifically when one of them is moist, but if both are dry, even rinsing is not required.`,
    '2#א': `Lest one eat. Taz wrote — and one may challenge: Beit Yosef wrote in siman 2 that wherever b'dieved is permitted we are not concerned initially lest one forget, and this is proven in many places; if so why are we concerned here lest one eat without rinsing? And one may answer that where there is no clear prohibition before us but only a doubt whether there is a prohibition requiring investigation afterward — there we say since b'dieved it is permitted, one may rely l'chatchila on the investigation he will do afterward; unlike here where there is a clear prohibition that one will salt meat in a forbidden vessel, only that it has a remedy afterward through rinsing — we are properly concerned lest one forget to rinse; and Maharshal wrote that travelers who eat salted herring on gentile vessels do not act well, for rinsing is required; and all the more those who buy salted cucumber types called ogrekes with brine and eat in their bowl, for even the cucumbers require rinsing if placed in their bowl; if so the brine, to which rinsing does not apply, is all forbidden; and Terumat HaChacham wrote in time of pressure, such as lodging in a gentile house, it is considered b'dieved — end of his words.`,
    '2#ב': `And specifically. Behold Shach disagrees with this hagahah and wrote that Rama's words in Darkei Moshe too require study — he learned this law from what Orach Chaim wrote that gentile vessels that were rinsed well are permitted to place spices or onion and other sharp things that do not extract a hard dry thing in dry — end of his words; and from Ba'al HaItur's words it does not appear so, only spices and the like — what is not customary to rinse is forbidden to place in a forbidden vessel; and from this he distinguishes that a forbidden vessel used hot is forbidden to use with permitted cold use, and so Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 121; however if forbidden cold use was used, permitted cold use is permitted even l'chatchila, and so we find with chametz vessels that one may use matzah — it also deals in such a case as Orach Chaim siman 451; or one may distinguish whether the vessel was washed well initially or not — if washed well initially it is certainly permitted; and thus Ba'er Hetev's view that although the vessel is forbidden because of absorption of prohibition, nevertheless since prohibition was not absorbed through hot use only through pickling and salting and the like, permitted cold use is permitted; but his words are astonishing at the outset in distinguishing whether prohibition was absorbed through hot or not — this is not so, for it is explained in siman 121 to the contrary that even if absorbed through hot, permitted cold use is permitted; and likewise regarding chametz — what difference whether absorbed through cold or hot if one now uses cold; and in the Gemara they distinguish between hot use and cold use only for using it now hot, but for cold use wiping the vessel alone suffices; and likewise siman 69 regarding salting in an unperforated vessel that one may eat afterward cold — he brought there one view that even if a boiling vessel was used the law is so; also what Darkei Moshe distinguishes between moist permitted and dry is not so, for Rashba explicitly permits eating cold even moist; but in truth it appears to me that what Orach Chaim wrote — gentile vessels that were rinsed well — deals where we know certainly, such as that a Jew rinsed now, and it does not mean that rinsing specifically is required, for the poskim who learned this law hold that thorough wiping everywhere is like rinsing; and Ba'al HaItur deals without wiping; or rather the main point is that earthenware is different, forbidden to use even cold, and Taz follow their reasons that even in earthenware permitted cold use is permitted; and what Orach Chaim wrote that it does not extract a hard dry thing in dry — is to exclude spices and other sharp moist things; but here, that it does not extract a hard thing, even moist; however all this is occasional use, but to use them regularly even cold is forbidden — a decree lest one use them hot, as Rama wrote in hagahah siman 121 s.k. 5 — end of his words; and Taz emends Rama's words and it should read: but a completely dry thing, or if the vessel did not absorb only through cold use, it is permitted to place moist food without rinsing at all; and the explanation of his words is that he refers to what Shulchan Aruch wrote regarding a forbidden cold bowl, namely that hot use was used; and on this he said that with a dry thing it is permitted to use without rinsing, and likewise if forbidden use was not used initially only cold, afterward permitted without rinsing even for moist food — end of his words.`,
    '3#_': `Cheese. Explanation: without rinsing, and we are concerned lest one forget and eat without rinsing; and it was already explained in seif 1 that specifically when one of them is moist.`,
    '4#א': `Prevails. Shach wrote that for us even hot roasted meat outside the pot is also forbidden; and Taz wrote all this is specifically regarding prohibition because of itself, but if it is absorbed prohibition it does not pass from piece to piece without gravy — see siman 105 s.k. 7 (and in Shenei Luchot in the name of Maharshal it appears to him when the prohibition is hot and the permitted cold placed next to each other — all are forbidden; and if the prohibition is cold and the permitted hot — one should be stringent per Rashba to require peeling — end of his words).`,
    '4#ב': `One peels. Because the lower is cold it cools the upper, except that it absorbed a little; therefore peeling is required; and above siman 105 s.k. 3 it is explained that even if hot milk fell on cold meat through pouring without interrupting the stream, peeling suffices; and likewise even if the stream was interrupted, peeling is required — see there.`,
    '4#ג': `B'dieved. [Riva, Maharshal, Maharai, and Taz agreed with them that 60 is required in the milk against the peel forbidden from it] and Shach wonders at this and brings several proofs to refute this law; and Rama himself wrote in siman 98 s.k. 4 that a vessel forbidden through salting the measure of peeling requires afterward sixty; and therefore it must deal here where the peel is not recognizable, namely something blended; but if recognizable and peeling applies, if they did not peel, sixty is required against the peel — see there at length.`,
    '4#ד': `And permitted. Orach Chaim wrote in the name of Mordechai and Semag that specifically in basar b'chalav the law is so, but in other prohibitions even if both are cold, peeling is required; and Shach challenged this — it requires study, for this is not found in Mordechai and Semag, and on the contrary from the conclusion of their words it implies the opposite — see there; and from Taz's words it implies one should be stringent also in basar b'chalav if one did not peel to require sixty against the peel; and he wrote that so appears to him for practical law.`,
    '5#א': `And it waited. And that which in siman 69 s.k. 20 it is explained that even beforehand it is hot, and somewhat implies there that after waiting the measure of salting it is not hot — it must be that it is different there, for it deals with blood prohibition, and therefore blood and salt are hot at the time of salting, but not when it waited the measure of salting, when the strength of the salt ceased because it expelled all the blood; but here it deals with meat and cheese prohibition — as long as it was not salted properly the meat is not hot, but when it waited the measure of salting it is considered hot, for the strength of the salt did not cease since it expelled nothing; certainly it deals that the meat was already salted and rinsed as required and now was salted again, for otherwise the cheese would be forbidden because of blood — end of his words.`,
    '5#ב': `And some say. Behold Shach challenged this hagahah and his view is that the Mechaber was not precise at all in comparing meat and cheese to blood prohibition and followed Terumat HaDeshen siman 159 — there the case was salting blood prohibition in geese where one was found treifah, etc., and he permitted with great loss and a mitzvah meal; but that case is not comparable here, for there it appeared to permit even without great loss because regarding blood prohibition we say that after waiting the measure of salting the strength of the salt already ceased; but since from other poskim's words regarding Rashi's incident it implies that even regarding blood we do not say the strength of the salt ceased after waiting the measure of salting — if so do not add — not to permit except with great loss and mitzvah meal; unlike here regarding meat and cheese where the salt expelled nothing — it is obvious that even after the measure of salting it is considered hot; and he wrote that he proposed this to the Gaon our master and teacher and other great teachers and they agreed to his words. However within the measure of salting it is forbidden forever (even regarding blood prohibition) and even with great loss and mitzvah meal, for Ran's view that before the measure of salting it is not like hot is singular against all poskim — end of his words (and Peri Chadash wrote one must not be lenient at all even with great loss and mitzvah meal, since Ra'avad, Ramban, Rashba, Ra'ah, R' Yitzchak, and Ran hold we never say the strength of the salt ceased even if it stayed in its salt a full day or more — who will be lenient against them even with great loss — see there).`,
    '5#ג': `Upper. Shach wrote — and although in siman 70 s.k. 4 and siman 105 s.k. 11 he ruled to distinguish in salted meat between upper and lower — it must be specifically to forbid all of it, namely in fatty meat there is a difference, but to forbid the measure of peeling such as in lean meat there is no difference, for although the lower prevails, nevertheless peeling is required; however in Beit Yosef this siman it does not appear so — investigate.`,
    '5#ד': `To peel. (*) These circled words are not in the old edition. Shach wrote it is not relevant here to say since it is busy expelling it does not absorb, or as in other places that specifically regarding blood we say this and not regarding other prohibitions.`,
    '5#ה': `And both. Terumat HaChacham wrote — this is if only the meat was salted, but if only the milk was salted the milk is permitted; and Shach on this wrote it requires study for practical law, since Mordechai and Semag forbid — who will tell us to be lenient; also Maharshal wrote, etc., and it requires study, for in Semag it is forbidden; and in Hagahot Shach that Beit Yosef brought in siman 70 it implies soft milk that is not congealed is not called a clear substance, and so Maharshal.`,
    '5#ו': `Completely. Explanation: even if salted, all is permitted (for it is as if they were not salted since they already dried from their salting — Terumat HaChacham and Shach; and Peri Chadash wrote per what is written above that we do not say the strength of the salt ceased — if so when it becomes moist again it revives and absorbs from the salt next to it and one should forbid even if completely dry — and so is primary).`,
    '5#ז': `One. Shach explained that if it fell on the entire vessel peeling does not apply, for the vessel would be ruined, or great effort is required; and therefore if there is no loss and one wishes to exert himself it is permitted (for salted gravy is not absorbed more than the measure of peeling); and although in siman 121 s.k. 2 and Orach Chaim siman 451 s.k. 16 Rama wrote in hagahah that wherever hag'alah is required, if one peeled the vessel it does not help — there it deals with something absorbed through hot use and the like that is absorbed in the entire vessel; but salted gravy is not absorbed except the measure of peeling, therefore peeling suffices.`,
    '6#א': `Fatty. Explanation: either the meat or the cheese.`,
    '6#ב': `105. For there it is explained that for us there is no difference between lean and fatty, and all salting we estimate at sixty — see there.`,
    '7#א': `Its salt. Shach wrote that even if it was not salted at all, peeling is also required, like the law of hot into cold that requires peeling; and although the roast sinks into the milk and the milk floats on the roast, it is not like cold into hot, for whatever is in its place prevails and cools; therefore peeling suffices.`,
    '7#ב': `Baked. Explanation: baked and cooked are considered like roasted — one says hot, one says cold.`,
    '7#ג': `Thus. And the reason is that since it was roasted or baked or cooked, although it is cold it is soft and absorbs, and there is no difference between slightly salted that is eaten because of its salt and not salted at all — forever in cold roast the law is thus; nevertheless the milk is permitted and we do not say the roast or baked or cooked expels.`,
    '7#ד': `Great loss. Shach wrote it requires study whether to be lenient with great loss, since many poskim forbid even live meat; and Taz wrote that from Rama's words here it implies that with great loss one may be lenient per the first view that is lenient regarding eaten because of its salt — all that is not hot roast; and above in seif 5 Rama wrote we are not expert and should forbid even salting of roast without great loss; and it must be that we do not combine two leniencies for the sake of great loss — namely that salting of roast is not called not eaten because of its salt, and also to rely on the first view here that we do not forbid live meat with cracks except in hot roast; we do not do so thus; however in hot roast that is not seasoned and has no cracks, peeling suffices — and so is primary; however all this when hot roast fell into milk, but if milk or other prohibition fell on hot roast, its law will be explained in siman 92 and siman 105.`,
    '8#_': `Pickled. (And if there is a doubt whether salted, it is permitted to eat, for we say regarding pickling siman 105 seif 1 in hagahah "doubt pickling" we follow leniency per Bach, because from the Torah it is forbidden only through cooking — if so here too regarding salting the law is so — Beit Yehudah.)`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Mishnah Chullin daf 107 and as Abaye explains there — Rashba.`,
    '2#_': `Rashba and Tur in the name of Ba'al HaItur — from that which we learned "provided they do not touch each other."`,
    '3#_': `Tur, and there he wrote "raw meat"; and see siman 89 seif 4 and what I noted there.`,
    '4#א': `The dispute of Rav and Shmuel Pesachim daf 76 and as Shmuel — the baraita there — the agreement of the poskim.`,
    '4#ב': `Baraitot there and as they are resolved there in the Gemara.`,
    '5#א': `Rava's statement according to Shmuel that salted meat is like hot — Chullin daf 112 and Pesachim daf 76.`,
    '5#ב': `Tur in the name of Rabbenu Tam (and Ran), and so Tosafot, Rosh, and Mordechai in his name, and so is the view of all of them and other poskim.`,
    '5#ג': `There in the name of Rashi's explanation of Rava's statement above, and Ran.`,
    '5#ד': `Tosafot and Rosh there, and so Tur; and see siman 105.`,
    '5#ה': `Tur — from that which impure salted and pure unsalted, etc. — there daf 113.`,
    '5#ו': `There, and so Semak and Mordechai there and Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 9.`,
    '6#_': `There and siman 105 — since we say salted meat is like hot, roasted meat is compared to roasted meat for all its matters.`,
    '7#_': `There daf 112 — in the incident of the young dove.`,
    '8#_': `Hagahot Ashiri end of Avodah Zarah and Mordechai there chapter 2, and so Agur in his name.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '2#א': `Everything, etc. — as written in the Mishnah "provided, etc." and the Gemara says "let it suffice," etc., and this is what "and specifically," etc. means: (Lekut) Everything, etc., and specifically, etc. — on Rashi Chullin 8a s.v. "let it suffice," etc. — he explains forbidden and not permitted, for the language of "let it suffice" implies forbidden and not permitted; and that he did not challenge on permission is because Abaye could not challenge that he did not mention permission l'chatchila and slaughtering is also unnecessary, lest one say he holds rinsing is required even for roasted meat and rinsing is not needed for roasted meat because of blood on the surface unlike meat — see above siman 120 s.k. 12 (end).`,
    '2#ב': `(Lekut) And specifically, etc. — in Me'iri he brought proof from "they placed the fats on the breasts" and Tosafot 8b s.v. "not," etc. — it is not proof, for from the Torah it is certainly permitted; furthermore priests are zealous; and Ra'ah disagreed there 112a (end). (Lekut) And specifically, etc. — from the challenge in Chullin 8b "and it was fixed one," etc., and Rashi: fats in the market to sell; and Hagahot Ashiri there s.v. "from here," etc. — his reason is that because of scoring the meat, rinsing suffices, for milk is lean; and as written siman 64 — see there unlike actual milk — (end).`,
    '2#ג': `And specifically a thing, etc. — on Tosafot Avodah Zarah 32a s.v. "but," and from here Rabbenu Tam, etc.`,
    '4#_': `And in a place where the meat, etc. — Shach means if the peel blended and is not recognizable, similar to milk that permits in Shulchan Aruch, and per Rabbenu Tam's answer in Tosafot there s.v. "it teaches," and see above siman 69 s.k. 16 in hagahah, and if used, etc., as Riva's view.`,
    '5#א': `That is, like the salting, etc. — Tosafot in Chullin there and Menachot 21a — from that which we say there that blood that was cooked does not act upon it, and likewise blood that was salted; and he said to them mutually that both have one reason because salted is like hot; and as Rashi wrote there; and he challenges there "rather the verse limited it," etc. — thus salting of kodshim is not eaten because of its salt; and it says there "and likewise for a pot": (Lekut) That is, like the salting, etc. — on Tosafot Chullin there s.v. "this is only," etc., and it is difficult for Rabbenu Tam, etc., and likewise what it says there 113a — Shmuel said one may not place meat, etc.; Rav Sheshet salted, etc.; what is the reason, etc.; and there 112b after the fish rested, etc. — thus even within this measure it forbids, and within this measure it is certainly not so salted — Taz (end).`,
    '5#ב': `And it waited, etc. — Ran, and as written in Ba'al HaGuf that the measure of salting is enough to roast, for with that it is considered hot as roasted; and in Rashba's view even though the meat is eaten because of its salt, the gravy and blood are not eaten because of their salt immediately, and even the measure of enough to put on fire and boil is not required, only that it be pickled; but regarding salted meat the same applies immediately; and as Rosh wrote in chapter 25 — see siman 69 s.k. 18 and Shach: (Lekut) And it waited, etc. — likewise Rif, and likewise in Terumat HaDeshen 72b in the name of R' Yitzchak b'Ritzteloni, but he disagrees with what is written 112b after the fish rested, etc. — thus within the measure birds exude, etc. — Ran rejected this (end); and see above siman 69 s.k. 18 and 20 (end).`,
    '5#ג': `And some say that after, etc. — Tosafot 112b s.v. "and fish." An incident occurred in his house, etc., unless one must say, etc., or because the strength of the salt ceased, etc.; and behold most poskim disagree with this reason, and this is what "for great need," etc. means, and Shach.`,
    '5#ד': `Even if not, etc. — see above siman 69 s.k. 4 in Shulchan Aruch, and if not, etc.`,
    '5#ה': `But anything that, etc. — Gemara there.`,
    '5#ו': `And salted like the salting, etc. — per Rashi's explanation there and there: (Lekut) And salted like the salting, etc. — even, etc. — so Ra'avad inferred from "because of its salt" and there is no contradiction with "its salt" (end).`,
    '5#ז': `And there is no difference. Tosafot 113a s.v. "pure."`,
    '5#ח': `Therefore, etc. — the law of salted meat that forbids the measure of peeling as written (107b) — though peeling is not required, in such a case peeling is required, like touching earthenware of an oven (Pesachim 76b in the Mishnah) and slaughtering with a borrowed knife (Chullin 8b), and even per those who require removal as Rosh wrote in Pesachim.`,
    '5#ט': `And if one, etc. — Gemara there, and rinsing like the law of cold.`,
    '5#י': `And some say, etc. — as written in seif 6 "where we say," and we are not expert in this.`,
    '5#כ': `And gravy of, etc. — meaning if impossible through peeling, and as written; and if not, etc.`,
    '6#_': `Where we say, etc. — as written 97a, and the same for salted meat as written there 2 that it is like roasted, and this is what "and likewise its companion," etc. means; and as written that milk is different because it spreads and forbids the kid, and so here; and even the fatty piece is forbidden as Pesachim 76b "slaughtered meat that is fatty," etc., and even Levi did not explain except because he holds smell is not a matter (and see above siman 105 s.k. 55); and this is what "all of it is forbidden, and this, and all the more this" means.`,
    '7#א': `That which we distinguish, etc. — for roasted meat forbids the measure of peeling because of roast and not because of salted, since it is eaten because of its salt — unsalted is the same; and this is Rashba's view in Terumat HaDeshen, and he follows his view that hot roast only forbids the measure of peeling, except he wrote there is reason to be concerned for the view that requires removal; but for practical law he agreed to the first reason, and Abudraham in siman 105.`,
    '7#ב': `And some say even if. Likewise Tosafot, and per their view that roast forbids the measure of removal, and therefore in cold it deals; and on Tosafot 96b s.v. "until," etc.: (Lekut) And some say even if, etc. — and as written in Avodah Zarah 65b in the Mishnah and Gemara that split ones even in cold absorb immediately, and as I wrote siman 134 s.k. 8 and 11; and immediately absorbs, for if much time passed even on lentils and barley it is forbidden as written in siman 111; and it appears primary is Semak and Shulchan Paz that even live cold, all that has cracks is forbidden, and so I ruled, and see Orach Chaim siman 465 s.k. 3 (end).`,
    '8#_': `There is no basar b'chalav, etc. — see above siman 87 seif 1 in hagahah: (Lekut) There is no basar b'chalav, etc. — from the challenge in Avodah Zarah 35b between R' Chananel whether, etc., and he did not also challenge Shmuel because of basar b'chalav; Mordechai there explains responsum 1266, and he wrote there is room to reject, and see above siman 90 that milk of a slaughtered animal is only rabbinic, and all the more a carcass as I wrote siman 87 s.k. 6 (his words require study); and on Tosafot there 1a s.v. "because," etc. (end).`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Meat and cheese that touched each other are permitted except that rinsing is required, etc., and specifically when one of them is moist, but if both are dry even rinsing is not required. Bach. Shach s.k. 1. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Tur section 1; Peri Chadash section 1; Levush section 1; Beit Lechem Yehudah section 1; Kereti section 1; Mateh Yehonatan on Terumat HaChacham general rule 21 section 1; Berit Kehunah in Sha'ar 6 section 1; Chayei Adam general rule 42 section 1; Chochmat Adam section 1; Beit Yitzchak in Amud HaZahav section 1; and when both are salted, even if dry, rinsing is required as Shulchan Aruch writes below seif 5 — Chochmat Adam in Biurim section 2; and likewise sharp foods even if dry require rinsing — Chochmat Adam there and in Chidush section 1; Pit'hei Teshuva section 1; Beit Yitzchak there; Zivchei Tzedek section 1.`,
    '2#_': `(2) And if the meat is fatty, even though it has no moisture, presumably the fat congeals and is not recognizable and rinsing is required; and furthermore in cheese, even though dry, there is concern for many crumbs that adhere and rinsing is required — Peri To'ar section 1; Zechor LeAvraham section 2 of Chayei Adam on siman 91; Zivchei Tzedek section 2.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. Except that rinsing is required, etc. — this means plain rinsing without rubbing; but where there is knife pressure and greasiness we require rubbing until one knows by estimate that greasiness left him, such as cutting meat with a knife that cut cheese; but pressure without greasiness or greasiness without pressure is permitted with plain rinsing. Chochmat Adam section 2 and in Biur section 1; Or HaYashar section 1; Pit'hei Teshuva there; Beit Yitzchak there; Zivchei Tzedek section 3; and specifically milk that was not dissolved in water, but milk dissolved in water adheres more and requires great rubbing. Or HaYashar there. Pit'hei Teshuva there. Yad Ephraim on seif 2, and he wrote that great rubbing is required until one knows by estimate that the prohibition left him. Zivchei Tzedek there.`,
    '4#_': `(4) [Seif 2] Everything that requires rinsing, such as placing permitted cold meat in a forbidden cold bowl — forbidden l'chatchila, etc.; and the same to place forbidden cold in a permitted cold vessel that is not customary to rinse — forbidden l'chatchila; and if customary to rinse — permitted. Beit Yosef below siman 94 in s.v. "sheep"; Netiv 15 section 29. Shach s.k. 2 in the name of Rashba in Tur HaShalem long and short. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Tur section 2; Peri Chadash section 2; Levush section 3; Beit Lechem Yehudah section 3; Shulchan Petach David section 2; Beit Yitzchak section 2; Zivchei Tzedek section 4.`,
    '5#_': `(5) And that which we say it is forbidden l'chatchila to place cold meat in a forbidden cold bowl lest one eat without rinsing, etc. — this is in a vessel not rinsed initially; but in a metal vessel, if the forbidden vessel was rinsed initially, it is permitted l'chatchila to place cold meat there and the meat does not require rinsing. Shach s.k. 3. Peri Chadash section 3; Peri To'ar section 2; Kehalat Yehudah in this siman seif 2; responsum Batei Kehunah vol. 1 Beit Va'ad siman 18; Mishnat Zekenim end of section 3; Zivchei Tzedek section 5.`,
    '6#_': `(6) And if the vessel was wiped and not rinsed — some say wiping helps like rinsing, for certainly if the vessel was wiped in a way that has no greasiness at all, there is no reason to require rinsing. Shach there in the name of Ra'ah. And some say wiping does not help, for even if wiped well and one placed meat, the meat also requires rinsing, and l'chatchila it is forbidden to place in the said vessel that was wiped without rinsing; and specifically if the vessel was rinsed may one place meat. Batei Kehunah there daf 93 side 2 in the name of Rashba; and so Peri Chadash there — here it deals with a forbidden bowl that was wiped but not rinsed, and since rinsing is required, if used without rinsing one rinses what was used. Mishnat Zekenim there. Zivchei Tzedek section 6, and he wrote and so is primary.`,
    '7#_': `(7) And this is in a metal vessel — rinsing helps; but in earthenware rinsing does not help, and if used hot it is forbidden to use even cold — a decree lest one use it hot; and even if rinsed it is forbidden, for rinsing does not help for earthenware. Peri Chadash there. Peri To'ar there. Batei Kehunah there daf 93 side 4. Kehalat Yehudah there. Shulchan Petach David section 3; Or HaYashar section 2; Chayei Adam general rule 56 section 2; and if used b'dieved with permitted cold, even though the earthenware vessel was rinsed, one must rinse that permitted cold. Peri Chadash there. Peri To'ar there. Batei Kehunah there. Kehalat Yehudah there. However an earthenware vessel whose use is cold — permitted to use cold after rinsing the vessel like metal vessels, but not hot. Peri Chadash there. Peri To'ar there. Batei Kehunah there. Kehalat Yehudah there. Or HaYashar there. Zivchei Tzedek section 7.`,
    '8#_': `(8) And the same for a bone vessel that has no remedy in hag'alah that one is concerned for, or a wooden vessel that absorbed through fire when hot soft milk fell from a candle called kreuzil whose use is through fire without interrupting the stream — and the same for a candle made like a wick as ours — if the candle fell on a wooden vessel it is absorbed through fire and hag'alah does not suffice; if so even occasional use is forbidden like earthenware, for one reason — since hag'alah is impossible it comes to loss. Shulchan Petach David there; meaning both a bone vessel that has no remedy in hag'alah that we are concerned for, and a wooden vessel that absorbed through fire that also has no remedy in hag'alah and requires libun, and if impossible to burn its law is like earthenware and forbidden to use cold even after rinsing lest it come to loss to use them hot, as written in the noted words.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#_': `One must rinse if one of them is moist; but if both are dry, even rinsing is not required.`,
    '2#_': `And specifically — Shach and Peri Chadash elaborated greatly on this, and the essence of their words as Shach concluded in s.k. 3, or rather the main point is that an earthenware vessel is different, and Rashba too concedes regarding earthenware, etc.; and see Peleti who holds that Rama's intent regarding a meat vessel to put milk in it or the reverse — all agree that everything is judged like a rinsed vessel and it is permitted to use it; and the "forbidden" Rama mentioned is not precise, but deals only with meat regarding milk or the reverse.`,
    '3#_': `Forbidden to eat it, lest one forget and eat without rinsing; and it deals with something that has a little moisture, and if not — permitted.`,
    '4#א': `Hot meat — specifically in a pot; but on a spit it is forbidden only the measure of peeling.`,
    '4#ב': `One peels — because the upper prevails and it absorbed a little; nevertheless peeling is required.`,
    '4#ג': `Permitted — the conclusion of the poskim; not only meat into milk but the same law applies to permitted into forbidden — nevertheless the law is so.`,
    '4#ד': `And in the place where Peleti wrote — for Magen Avraham's view is specifically where peeling is required because the lower prevails, for then it absorbed a little — they are stringent to peel; in that case 60 is not required; but where by law peeling is required, the peel forbids like any other prohibition; and Shach, Peri Chadash, and Maharsha hold to distinguish between when the peel is recognizable and stands in place — then its taste forbids — unlike when the peel is not recognizable and does not stand in place — then it is not nullified; and I wrote to distinguish between when the permitted absorbed, as in basar b'chalav, and when the prohibition absorbed, as in all prohibitions; and see Peleti — investigate.`,
    '5#א': `And it waited — but before it waited it does not return to hot; and that which above siman 69 implies it is immediately considered hot — the rabbis distinguish between meat and gravy, for gravy is immediately hot; and Shach distinguishes regarding blood — the nature of salt for blood is immediately hot, but regarding the heat of the piece itself one needs the measure of salting; and see Peleti who proved not like Shach, only the Mechaber was stringent regarding a Torah prohibition even before the measure of salting, but in basar b'chalav, which is rabbinic salting, he was lenient until it waits the measure of salting — see there.`,
    '5#ב': `For great need, etc.; and within the measure of salting Shach's view is to forbid even where there is loss; and see Peleti — when it is within the measure of salting and was salted to extract blood, there is not so much heat to cook; but if its action is to extract blood it is immediately hot and one may not permit even with great loss; and likewise if it already expelled its blood and was salted again, even with loss; and see there what he wrote about gravy from meat — even within the measure of salting it forbids according to all, and great loss does not help.`,
    '5#ג': `That we are not expert — it appears specifically regarding nevelah and the like, which is a Torah doubt; but in basar b'chalav, which is a rabbinic doubt, it is permitted like nispach.`,
    '5#ד': `And there is no difference — certainly the Mechaber holds like Rashba that even in salted meat there is a difference between lower and upper prevails — but this is only in something fatty; but in lean meat, which forbids only the measure of peeling, there is no difference, for it does not forbid more than peeling; and in peeling, even though the lower is cold it absorbed a little — nevertheless peeling is required; therefore there is no difference.`,
    '5#ה': `Therefore — and we do not say as in other places, or "since it is busy expelling it does not absorb," etc. — we do not say this only regarding blood and not regarding other absorption, not like Orach Chaim general rule 12 at all — Shach; and see Peleti who ties it to the dispute of Rashi and Rashbam — see there.`,
    '5#ו': `On the entire vessel — peeling does not apply; but on part of the vessel one can peel and hag'alah is not required.`,
    '5#ז': `Nevertheless the gravy — see Peleti, the main reason is that we are not expert between eaten and not eaten because of salt — see there.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(siman 91 seif 5) Salting, etc., and it waited the measure of salting for a pot. See the Acharonim who challenged from that which is written above siman 69 that even beforehand it is also like hot; and one may answer that there is a difference between salted meat and gravy — gravy is hot beforehand and salted meat is not hot until it waits; and Pri Te'uar explains the reason — because meat cools the salt since it lies on it, unlike gravy which is collected in a vessel by itself and nothing cools it — it is immediately hot; or one may say as we say regarding Shabbat that salt is needed in a pot like ox meat — it implies that salt does not cook ox meat, and nevertheless it is forbidden to put hot meat in it — because even though the meat is not cooked, the moisture in it cooks, as Rosh wrote in Shabbat there; if so here too — the moisture which is gravy is immediately called hot, unlike meat which is not called hot until it waits the measure of salting. But one may ask — how does the Mechaber intend to permit before it waited, for once meat was salted with cheese there is certainly gravy here; and Shach who wrote to distinguish between gravy and salt — this is according to his view that after rinsing the openings of exudation closed and it will never expel anything, even gravy; and here it deals with meat already salted and rinsed and there is no gravy; however for poskim who hold that even after rinsing it still has exudation of gravy — it must deal with after all exudations of gravy finished as above; and one may say that gravy exuded through salting is not forbidden because of basar b'chalav, for in all prohibitions if not for the verse "the impure to forbid their gravy" we would say it is truly permitted; if so regarding meat which is permitted one may say regarding gravy there is no Torah taste at all, and there is some proof — if not, why is the verse of the impure needed; if so in gravy there is no Torah taste — if so it is not forbidden at all in basar b'chalav because of gravy — investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(there) And some say that after it waited in its salt the measure of salting, etc. See Shach s.k. 11 who wrote that the Mechaber followed Terumat HaDeshen siman 159 — see there; and I heard from my master and teacher that from Rosh's second version in resolving the incident of Rashi it proves that salt after waiting the measure of salting always stands in its heat — thus Rosh in chapter 25: "and he said we do not say salted is like hot except at the time of its exuding; if so at the time of completing exuding it is not considered hot, for regarding the dove that fell into brine we say salted is like hot, etc., and one may answer, etc.; and in section 38 he wrote: 'and meat that was salted and waited, etc., or the strength of the salt ceased, etc., and because of that salt it is no longer considered hot after completing exuding, etc., and it is not comparable to the dove, etc., that was considered hot if the brine was not eaten because of its salt, for there the strength of the salt did not cease' — and one may analyze his words — if so why did he challenge above that the vessel should be forbidden because of the salt dissolved, lest after completing exuding it is no longer called hot; and one cannot challenge from the dove, for there the strength of the salt did not cease as he wrote here; and it must be that Rosh holds that even though we say the strength of the salt ceased — meaning it is no longer considered strongly salted since its strength ceased in extracting blood from the meat — nevertheless gravy always stands in heat, for there is no language to say the strength of the salt ceased; if so Rosh's challenge is sound that meat should be forbidden because of blood-salt dissolved in gravy — as in brine, through the salt in it it is always considered hot, so too gravy that exuded before waiting the measure of waiting — through the salt in it it always stands in heat; therefore one should wonder at Terumat HaDeshen who permitted in that incident — for presumably it was not yet rinsed and presumably there was still gravy and it is always hot and treifah gravy was absorbed in kosher meat and one should forbid even with great loss; and one may say Rama brought his view here in basar b'chalav where otherwise there is reason to say gravy does not forbid in basar b'chalav as above — and although Rama holds below that gravy is like meat, here too it is on this reason alone he does not wish to rely; what is not so here — one may say he relied on Terumat HaDeshen because of this reason that gravy does not forbid in basar b'chalav; and this is somewhat clear from Rama bringing Terumat HaDeshen's law here regarding basar b'chalav where its place is not here — he should have brought it below regarding mixture of other prohibitions siman 105 — but certainly there he does not wish to rely on Terumat HaDeshen — as he wrote — investigate.`,
    '3#_': `(there seif 7) That which we distinguish, etc. Shach s.k. 24 is not precise, etc.; and it appears to me that here it deals with even if the roast is not so hot, nevertheless it is forbidden since the lower is a little salted.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The law of meat and milk that touched. In it are 8 seifim: Meat and cheese that touched each other are permitted, but one must rinse (hadichah) the place of their contact, and it is permitted to wring them in one cloth, and we are not concerned lest they touch each other.`,
    '2#main': `Everything that requires rinsing — such as placing permitted cold meat in a cold forbidden bowl — is forbidden l'chatchila, lest one eat without rinsing; and this applies specifically to cooked meat, which is not customary to rinse, but something customary to rinse — such as raw meat and the like — is permitted l'chatchila: {Rama: And specifically something that has a little moisture; but something completely dry — if the vessel did not absorb except through cold use — it is permitted to place dry food in it without rinsing at all (Da'at Yachid), and see below siman 122.}`,
    '3#main': `One must be careful that meat not touch bread, for if it touches it, it is forbidden to eat it with cheese; and likewise one must be careful that cheese not touch it, for if it touches it, it is forbidden to eat it with meat.`,
    '4#main': `Hot meat and milk that mixed together — and even cold meat into hot milk or cold milk into hot meat — all are forbidden, because the lower one prevails; but hot milk that fell on cold meat, or hot meat that fell into cold milk — one peels (kli'ah) the meat and the rest of the meat is permitted (and in a place where the meat requires peeling, if one did not peel it and cooked it thus, it is permitted b'dieved) (Arukh general rule 29), and all the milk is permitted (Rambam, R' Chananel, R' Yitzchak, and Rif), and if they fell into each other while cold, one rinses the meat and it is permitted.`,
    '5#main': `Salted meat that cannot be eaten because of its salt — that is, like the salting they salt for a pot — and it waited the time of salting for a pot, as long as it was not rinsed, it is not considered "not eaten because of its salt": {Rama: And some say that after it waited in its salt the measure of salting, it is no longer considered hot (Tosafot and Rosh chapter 25 and Semag for one version); and for great need, such as great loss and it is for the sake of a mitzvah meal, one may rely on those who are lenient, but otherwise one must not be lenient at all (Terumat HaDeshen siman 159); even if the meat was not salted on both sides but only on one side — all that was salted with salting that is not eaten because of its salt is considered hot (Beit Yosef in the name of responsum of Rashba siman 265); but anything not salted so much is considered cold even if salted on both sides; and some say we are not expert in the matter and we must consider even salting of roasted meat as hot (Arukh 30), and it is good to be stringent where there is no great loss:} and salting like the salting needed for roasting — even after it was rinsed — is still not eaten because of its salt, and as long as they were not soaked in water its law is like hot to forbid the measure of peeling, and there is no difference between upper salted and lower unsalted and lower salted and upper unsalted — always the salted absorbs in the unsalted and does not absorb from it; therefore salted meat and cheese that touched each other require peeling both at the place of contact, and if one is salted and the other unsalted, the salted is permitted with rinsing and the unsalted requires peeling: {Rama: And some say in every salting we estimate at 60 (most early and late authorities), and see below siman 105 how we practice; and that pure salted and impure unsalted is permitted — this is when the impure is dry; but if it is a clear substance, the pure is forbidden since it is not eaten because of its salt — it absorbs the prohibition next to it, and both are forbidden (so it appears in Mordechai and so wrote Hagahot Maimoniyot, Shach, Semag, and Hagahot Mordechai), and specifically when the pure is a little moist; but if it is completely dry, it does not absorb from a moist thing next to it.} And if they are dry — even if they are salted — or moist not because of salting, rinsing suffices: {Rama: All gravy from meat that was salted — even if it was not salted only for roasting — is considered hot (Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 9 in the name of Machzor Vitry, Shach, Hagahot Ashiri from Avodah Zarah, and Agudah chapter 25); therefore if gravy fell on cheese or on a vessel it forbids (Arukh), even in a place where the meat does not forbid because it is not considered hot — nevertheless the gravy is considered "not eaten because of its salt." And gravy of meat that forbids that fell on a vessel requires hag'alah, and if it is an earthenware vessel it requires breaking (Shach there and Orach Chaim); and if it fell only on one place in a wooden vessel and the like — one peels its place and that suffices (Orach Chaim and see above seif 4 hagahah 1):}`,
    '6#main': `Where we say it forbids only the measure of peeling — when none of the pieces is fatty, for if it were entirely fatty it is forbidden (see below siman 105 seif 11), and likewise its companion piece is entirely forbidden because the fat spreads (and see below siman 105).`,
    '7#main': `That which we distinguish between "eaten because of its salt" and "not eaten because of its salt" — these matters apply to raw meat; but hot roasted meat that fell into salted meat — even if it is eaten because of its salt — requires peeling (see s.k. 20), and if it has cracks or is seasoned with spices and it is hot roasted — all of it is forbidden: {Rama: And likewise baked and cooked (Maharai in Hagahot Shach, Hagahot Mordechai, Semak, and Maharshal); and some say even if they are cold the law is thus (Beit Yosef according to Rosh, Terumat HaDeshen, and Tur), and so one should practice if there is no great loss.}`,
    '8#main': `Basar b'chalav is not forbidden through salting (melicha) or pickling (kavush) except through eating, but not through benefit.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(siman 91 Shach s.k. 3) But in truth it appears to me that what Orach Chaim and Ba'al HaItur wrote, etc. — and it appears to me that Ba'al HaItaur deals with a non-Jewish vessel that we are concerned lest one use it hot and we decree to forbid; and Orach Chaim deals with a vessel that is not a non-Jewish vessel, for even in hot use it is permitted b'dieved as below siman 93, and they did not decree — investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(there Taz s.k. 3) All that he wrote here is not so and he did not plumb the depth of the matter; everything was already explained properly in Shach s.k. 2 — see there.`,
    '3#_': `(there Taz s.k. 7) He elaborated to disagree with the Mechaber; and according to Shach s.k. 8 in this siman and siman 69 s.k. 63 his words are rejected — see there.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '2#_': `And specifically, etc. — the Acharonim Shach and Peri Chadash elaborated greatly, and both intended the same regarding earthenware — one should be stringent even if customary to rinse, as Shach wrote s.k. 3: "or rather the main point is that earthenware is different," etc.; and in my humble opinion Rama's law is inferred from this without distinguishing, for Peri Chadash himself wrote therefore the distinction between earthenware and a rinsed vessel — there one can kasher and use cold, and we are not concerned lest it stay and come to stumbling through hot use, for one can kasher it; but earthenware has no remedy and is forbidden even cold lest one leave it and come to stumble; and this is the reason for concern with an absorbed-forbidden vessel; but in this siman it deals with placing meat in a milk vessel or the reverse, and the forbidden vessel mentioned is not precise — only regarding meat it is called forbidden here, and all agree it is permitted, for when one leaves meat or milk vessels and leaves them, why forbid placing cold — certainly cold is permitted like a rinsed vessel; and therefore Rama forbade cold lest one leave it — this applies to meat or milk vessels, and all is judged like a rinsed vessel and permitted; and this is Rama's reason, therefore he omitted this law distinguishing earthenware from other vessels.`,
    '4#_': `And in a place where the meat, etc. See Shach who challenged from Tosafot Pesachim that one who salted in the gid of the sciatic nerve and afterward when cooked requires 60 even against the peel forbidden through salting; and he elaborated, and Taz elaborated from many proofs; and Magen Avraham Orach Chaim siman 464 s.k. 64 explained — specifically hot into cold requires only peeling as a general stringency and does not forbid mixtures; but not where by law peeling is required; and Orach Chaim general rule 59 section 2 similarly; and I challenged why something forbidden through peeling does not forbid its mixture — Rabbenu Tam's view from Tosafot; and Shach distinguished between a peel that mixed and is not recognizable versus recognizable — Peri Chadash agreed; and I did not understand their reason — why if the prohibition itself mixed it does not forbid, but if only taste stands and not substance it forbids; and the question stands: what difference if recognizable or not; and if their view is rabbinic and they were not stringent except when prohibition is recognizable — we may say per Magen Avraham and Orach Chaim there is room; but we hold like Ra'avad as Shach wrote above siman 22 — if so it is well said that absorbed inside does not forbid; except the matter that something forbidden through peeling does not forbid its mixture — Tosafot Pesachim in Rabbenu Tam's name, strained that all these authorities in Tosafot hold noten ta'am ben noten ta'am in half a piece; and they disagreed on this and Tosafot did not mention Rabbenu Tam holds like Ra'avad; and in Pesachim itself where Tosafot brought Rabbenu Tam's law they wrote s.v. "he takes," etc. — it is astonishing why the gravy forbidden from it should forbid up to removal; unless to teach that by itself it is not forbidden; and Shach should not have brought distant proofs — investigate; therefore it appears the known law regarding noten ta'am ben noten ta'am in basar b'chalav is permitted but forbidden in other prohibitions — weak taste in basar b'chalav heter does not forbid in mixture unlike other prohibitions where there is at least prohibition, even weak taste; and Rashba regarding the heifer distinguishes permitted and forbidden vessels from this reason; and therefore it is well that Rabbenu Tam's law applies only in basar b'chalav, and this is correct in reasoning; and Orach Chaim perhaps also intended only basar b'chalav since Rambam holds roast is permitted from the Torah, and all the more this peeling through pouring; but Maharsha challenged there in Tosafot regarding earthenware and cold flour — why peel, for cold gravy from it is forbidden the measure of peeling; and the strain is why Tosafot preferred the girsa and did not write per Riva — resolves Maharsha's challenge; therefore it appeared to say: Tosafot and Rosh hold in Chullin that in the Temple even for peace-offerings we establish by Torah law min b'mino is nullified in majority; and if gravy peel is forbidden, certainly all gravy is majority and min b'mino nullified in majority; and from chapter 8 itself we learn all the more from vessels in which Pesach was roasted; and for those who do not hold so in the Temple, the old girsa suffices — why remove its place, peeling is enough since the peel is a small matter and does not spread more than peeling — why remove in Pesach and scoop in flour; and all the more if there is no 60 against the peel, forbidden; and Shmuel's challenge stands — why remove; and with this Rambam's smoothing in chapter 8 is resolved — he did not explain it deals with earthenware and hot flour per Shmuel whom we follow; and from this Rambam's view appears as I wrote, unlike Shach — see below siman 105 what is written there.`,
    '5#א': `And it waited the measure of salting for a pot, etc. — the language "and it waited" implies it did not wait and is not forbidden; and this is Ran's view and the Acharonim wondered how Mechaber ruled plainly like Ran that before the measure of salting it is not hot, when above siman 69 if salted in an unperforated vessel and waited only enough to put on fire, etc., the meat is entirely forbidden as pickled and beforehand forbids the measure of peeling as hot roast; and some want to say it forbids immediately because gravy is immediately hot — I did not understand what help even here — meat was salted with cheese, gravy from meat is hot and forbids cheese and vice versa, and all taste from piece to piece through salting is called gravy; and more reasonable is Shach's view that regarding blood it is hotter than other taste, therefore salt draws blood and mixes and is immediately hot — see Shach s.k. 11; except I do not know one thing — if so we say before the measure of salting it is called eaten because of its salt and nevertheless regarding blood it is hot and absorbs — whence does Rabbenu Tam prove salting for a pot is not eaten because of its salt, for salting kodshim is like pot salting and in Menachot we say impossible to salt kodshim blood, for then it would be blood that was salted and like roast — investigate; and truly it requires study per Ran in the name of R' Yitzchak b'Ritzteloni that before the measure of salting it is not called hot; if so Rabbenu Tam's challenge stands regarding kodshim blood — how is it like pickled and like roast if it did not wait the measure of salting, for it is salted and offered immediately on the altar ramp; as explained in the Gemara and Rambam and Ran himself in chapter 35 that in kodshim it did not wait in salting, only immediately offered after salting — investigate; and what I said — proof from the Gemara if we say kodshim blood requires salting, kodshim would require salting on Yom Kippur too, etc. — investigate; nevertheless the challenge from fish and birds remains; and Ran's view appears that Mechaber holds there are divided views among authorities as the Acharonim wrote; regarding blood, d'oraisa, Mechaber was stringent immediately like hot roast per most authorities who hold so, unlike meat and cheese which is only rabbinic salting — he was lenient to rely on those who hold before the measure it is not hot roast; this appears Mechaber's intent to me; but Ran's view does not appear Mechaber's intent, for besides difficulty above, Ran also challenged from fish and birds — "since in pot measure it is called hot while salting on them, because their scales softened they are called hot beforehand" — I did not understand why; and because their scales softened they hurry to expel — what relation to salt's heat more than birds' heat; and why the Gemara said because scales softened they hurry to expel — the main point is missing, it should have said because scales softened they are hotter than birds; therefore it appears Ran certainly holds immediately hot, only not when salt acts otherwise; but when salt acts to extract blood it does not heat and cannot do two actions at once — therefore R' Yitzchak b'Ritzteloni requires the measure of salting for meat salted to extract its blood; and until the measure the salt is busy extracting blood; but after the measure when blood extraction finished, then it heats the piece — therefore regarding kodshim blood, since there the salt only heats, it is immediately hot as roast; and Ran explains fish similarly — end of his words; and with this all challenges are resolved; see Terumat HaDeshen 72b as I wrote — R' Yitzchak b'Ritzteloni deals with a piece salted to extract blood, and Rashba challenged Ran's challenge, and Ran answered as above; and this is clear to me — if so in meat and cheese there is no salt action to extract blood, it immediately heats and forbids; and it is clear one should be stringent even though rabbinic prohibition.`,
    '5#ב': `And for great need, etc. — the Acharonim raised that if meat already expelled blood, even with loss one may not permit, for Rosh wrote where the strength of salt did not cease, etc.; and since blood already expelled, how does salt's strength cease; and the Acharonim do not hold that exudation is mainly blood and gravy is minor; and I add even if salted to extract blood and there is gravy exuding within the measure of salting, this forbids even after long waiting, for regarding exuded gravy we do not say the strength of salt ceased; and Rosh's words require saying the same regarding gravy; and Rashi also appears to hold pot salting is hot, proven from kodshim blood; and one more proof — only within the measure of salting and not afterward, as in Rashi's incident above siman 69 s.k. 4; and the challenge from "hot" implies those not eaten because of salt are still hot — Rashi does not hold the strength of salt ceased here; and Rashi's challenge from "hot" stands; and Rashi resolved the Gemara's "hot" means not eaten because of salt — forbidden forever as salted for roasting; and without waiting Rashi agrees even pot salting forbids; and all Tosafot's challenges on him are resolved — investigate, and there is no distinction here.`,
    '5#ג': `And some say we are not expert, etc. — Orach Chaim wrote this regarding an unperforated vessel — see Orach Chaim general rule 70; and likewise regarding nevelah meat salted with shechted meat — there is Torah prohibition, therefore since we are not expert we forbid; but in basar b'chalav, rabbinic prohibition, they never said we are not expert; and what is compared to nispach — although one may distinguish rabbinic doubt of missing knowledge — nevertheless do not add; they were stringent only in Torah prohibition, and if another side to be lenient, be lenient in rabbinic matter, and all the more fowl which is doubly rabbinic.`,
    '5#ד': `Therefore meat and cheese — Shach wrote we do not say since busy expelling it does not absorb, etc. — only regarding blood, not like Orach Chaim general rule 12 section 11; his intent: Orach Chaim wrote therefore we do not say since cheese moisture is forbidden, in Torah prohibition we are stringent; and likewise beginning general rule 12 implies in rabbinic prohibition like fowl in milk one may be lenient; and truly Rashba's dispute with Rashi in chapter 25 112b — per master who salted shechted meat with treifah meat came before Rava — "the impure to forbid their gravy" — Rashi explained R' Meir was unsure whether we say this here too, only regarding blood; Rashba challenged — if so why was the baraita clear, for the doubt was only whether absorbed, therefore Rashba explained the doubt was whether gravy itself forbids; and Rashi did not want Rashba's explanation, for if he did not know how many mishnayot gravy forbids, all the more treifah; therefore Rashi explained he was unsure whether we say since busy, etc. — these are Torah prohibitions, clearly doubt does not emerge, only he holds all gravy is sweat and forbidden only rabbinically like fish gravy; therefore he was unsure whether doubt leans lenient; therefore Scripture answered "the impure to forbid their gravy" — then it is Torah prohibition; and if so Rashi's words are clear without strain; and per my view in rabbinic prohibition one may lean lenient with other branches; and one may challenge Orach Chaim — what Torah prohibition is here, salting and pickling in basar b'chalav are not Torah except through cooking; one may say his intent: live meat expelling moisture absorbed through salting moistens cheese — true it is not Torah for moisture in its innards, but the end will be meat cooked and not otherwise — then cooked with milk taste is Torah prohibition; and even what is forbidden through peeling when cooked does not forbid its mixture — one may say it spreads more than peeling, and in that peel there is Torah prohibition; and he holds salting at 60 as known — investigate.`,
    '5#ה': `Nevertheless gravy, etc. — nevertheless it requires study if lenient with great loss, since many forbid even live; and Taz implies Rama here permits with great loss per first view lenient regarding eaten because of salt; and above Rama wrote we are not expert to forbid salting roast without great loss; we do not combine two leniencies for great loss; however in hot roast without seasoning and cracks, peeling suffices — primary; however all when hot roast fell into milk; if milk or other prohibition on hot roast — siman 92 and 105; and the main reason in Orach Chaim is we are not expert between eaten and not eaten — therefore requires study.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `To rinse. Avodat HaTaharah in the name of Bach; however a sharp thing even if dry is like cold moist and requires rinsing, as written in seif 5; and if they are dry, etc. — likewise Peri Megadim and Chavat Da'at; and this rinsing means plain rinsing without rubbing; but permitted that touched forbidden grease requires rubbing until one knows by estimate that greasiness left him — so appears Peri Megadim in Shach (and see s.k. 3); but Chayei Adam wrote specifically knife pressure and greasiness require rubbing, such as cutting meat with a knife that cut cheese; but pressure without greasiness or greasiness without pressure suffices with plain rinsing; and see responsum Beit Ephraim Yoreh De'ah siman 29 that his view too is that greasiness without pressure suffices with plain rinsing; but he wrote specifically milk that was not dissolved in water, but milk dissolved in water — Rashba wrote it adheres more and requires great rubbing; therefore above siman 64 he forbids rinsing in a vessel used to rinse cheeses though there is no pressure; and he further wrote that thick milk or butter that fell on cold meat and congealed there requires great rubbing — see there; and a soft thing like cucumbers siman 96 seif 5 and the like requires scraping; and a thing that cannot be rinsed at all, such as water — see s.k. 21 afterward what its law is; and if salted or roasted without rinsing and something the measure of a peel was absorbed on the surface, nevertheless permitted without peeling; and likewise for other prohibitions a slight amount is permitted even in a moist thing that cannot be rinsed at all — end of his words.`,
    '2#_': `Lest one eat. Avodat HaTaharah in the name of Taz — what he wrote, and if so the brine, etc.; and see there that he wrote that Taz's intent — since one did so intentionally, all became forbidden to nullify or for one for whom it was nullified, as below siman 99; but if placed therein unintentionally (or for any other person) it is certainly permitted, for it is nullified in its minority — see there; and what Avodat HaTaharah wrote "in time of pressure," etc. — nevertheless if possible to wash the vessel well before use.`,
    '3#_': `Customary to rinse. See Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot Zahav end s.k. 1 that he concludes specifically placing in a forbidden wiped vessel is permitted if customary to rinse; but on actual forbidden substance, even if not greasy, it is forbidden and requires great rubbing; therefore Tur brought Ba'al HaItur's words regarding vessels — see there; and see Shach here s.k. 4 and below siman 105 s.k. 5 — it is explained there is no distinction, and even on actual forbidden substance it is permitted if customary to rinse, for rubbing is not required, as he himself wrote in Shulchan Petach David that I brought above section 1 — specifically if forbidden grease was touched requires rubbing; and Chavat Da'at and responsum Beit Ephraim disagree as written there; and specifically where there is knife pressure and greasiness requires rubbing; and from Tur's words who wrote Ba'al HaItur's words regarding vessels — there is no proof he means that if not customary to rinse, even on a forbidden vessel, but if customary to rinse one may say even on actual forbidden substance it is permitted; and Tur mentioned the vessel only initially and not at the end — see there; and see responsum Radbaz Chadashot siman 295.`,
    '4#_': `With cheese. Avodat HaTaharah, and this is Shach's words; and Peri Megadim who brought that Knesset HaGedolah wondered at Shach, for in Tur and Shulchan Aruch it implies there is no remedy through rinsing in bread that adheres strongly, since they wrote that if touched it is forbidden to eat with cheese; and Peri Megadim wrote that thereby Taz's "one must be careful," etc. is resolved — as it appears there are two concerns: lest one eat with cheese, and lest one forget to rinse; and whence do we learn that if we say rinsing does not help in bread it is resolved — investigate; and in my humble opinion it rather appears from Tur and Mechaber's language that Shach's view is somewhat implied, for they first wrote Ba'al HaItur's words that everything requiring rinsing is forbidden l'chatchila, then wrote this law "one must be careful," etc., and did not write immediately after seif 1 — it implies this law depends on seif 2; and more clear in Tur's language — see there; and what is written that from Taz's words it implies there is no remedy in bread that adheres strongly can be rejected — for certainly even if it adheres strongly, scraping certainly helps like cucumbers siman 96 seif 5; if so what Taz wrote "forbidden to eat with cheese" means it is permitted to eat through scraping; therefore their intent is forbidden to eat with cheese without any remedy; if so also no proof that scraping is not required and rinsing suffices per Shach; nevertheless it appears there is a difference in bread whether touched on whole bread or sliced outside on the hard side rinsing suffices, but if touched inside on the soft part scraping is required as s.k. 1; and what Peri Megadim wrote "thereby it is resolved," etc. — is not so, for even if rinsing does not suffice, scraping helps, and therefore there are always two concerns; however in truth there is difficulty — for this doubt "lest he not eat with cheese" is not considered a doubt at all, as Shach wrote siman 97 s.k. 2, because it is customary to eat bread with all kinds of foods.`,
    '5#_': `B'dieved. Avodat HaTaharah; and see responsum Teshuvot Chen siman 11 who elaborated and ruled that wherever the stream was not interrupted it is forbidden by law and 60 is required against the peel; but where pouring with interrupted stream one may be lenient, especially in basar b'chalav where wherever there is no cooking it is rabbinic; and in other prohibitions one should not be lenient except with great loss — see there further.`,
    '6#_': `See responsum Chavat Shalom siman 80 who wrote it is not difficult from below siman 104 s.k. 2 in hagahah — there it deals where they heated the fat gently and it is known the stream always touches the bottom of the vessel and always touches in its boiling through absorption, and the entire stream is its peel, for each drop that touches absorption pushes its fellow and it too touches, etc.; however here it deals with hot milk that fell at once — much pouring on the meat and rose on all its edges — the bottom of the milk touches the meat and the other part did not touch, and peeling is impossible here — therefore Riva and Rabbenu Tam permitted, and we learn the leniency that fell in basar b'chalav, and all the more in other prohibitions that are lighter; and it teaches the stringency of heating in other prohibitions, and all the more in basar b'chalav — see there.`,
    '7#_': `Clear substance. See responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 344 and responsum Noda BiYehudah Tinyana Yoreh De'ah siman 41 — what he wrote to resolve why the clear thing does not cool the salted thing — why should it lose the final rinsing that even once is not considered hot anymore — see there.`,
    '8#_': `In a wooden vessel and the like — peel. It implies that for earthenware peeling does not help; and see what is written on this above siman 69 s.k. 37.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(siman 91 Shach s.k. 1) Specifically when one of them is moist; and when salted, rinsing is required as below seif 6.`,
    '2#_': `(seif 2) But something customary to rinse. Ra'ah's view in s.v. "specifically to place forbidden in a permitted vessel" — permitted where its custom is to rinse the vessel; but with two types of food that touched each other — forbidden in every case; and Rashba in Ma'adanei HaMelekh wrote that this too is permitted.`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 3) In every case one should forbid. It is not understood — for in truth Tur forbids placing in a permitted vessel lest one not rinse; and perhaps Taz's intent per his words in s.k. 1 is regarding a vessel that was rinsed, where one could distinguish between meat and cheese and between vessels; but these deal with without rinsing and the forbidden substance is on the surface, like meat and cheese; and what did Ba'al HaItur innovate, and what Taz wrote that certainly in every case it is forbidden — meaning it is obvious in every case, namely meat and cheese, and likewise a vessel, for there is no leniency here more than meat and cheese.`,
    '4#_': `(Avodat HaTaharah) Even matzah in hot use. And I have difficulty — if so how did Terumat HaChacham write to resolve that Orach Chaim distinguishes between a vessel used hot and one used cold, namely that Orach Chaim deals with a gentile vessel whose use is cold — if so why did he write that it is permitted to place spices that do not extract a hard dry thing in dry — for even hot that extracts is permitted to use; investigate.`,
    '5#_': `(seif 4) And if they fell into each other while cold, one rinses the meat; and b'dieved if one did not rinse the meat and cooked it, 60 is required against all the meat; for specifically dry with dry does not require rinsing only because of concern for contact and adhesion on the surface — in that case it is permitted b'dieved; and likewise hot meat that fell requires peeling; nevertheless after peeling, thorough rinsing is also required — Noda BiYehudah (general rules 22 and 23 section 11; and see Peri Megadim siman 105 in Mishbetzot Zahav s.k. 2).`,
    '6#_': `(Taz s.k. 5) From piece to piece without gravy. It is astonishing — for here there is gravy since they are below and the milk prevails, which is called gravy.`,
    '7#_': `(Shach s.k. 8) And it appears there is no contradiction. And in Magen Avraham (siman 467 s.k. 133) he distinguishes that here specifically when the lower prevails, the peel is only a general stringency because it absorbed a little — in that case we are lenient b'dieved; but not in general — see there; and specifically when the stream was interrupted; but milk that fell on meat without interrupting the stream — perhaps even 60 does not help, for everything the stream touches is constantly forbidden, each drop and drop — and likewise below siman 104 regarding a stream of hot fat on a mouse; and see Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot Zahav (siman 105 s.k. 6); however according to Kapos Temarim (siman 104), that which we say there regarding fat, that with great loss we rule even in moist there is no noten ta'am ben noten ta'am because fat congeals immediately and is like dry — see there; if so with milk streaming on meat, 60 helps, for in moist there is no piece-not-piece; nevertheless Rama ruled below (siman 92) that in basar b'chalav even in moist we say noten ta'am ben noten ta'am, and it implies even in basar b'chalav which is rabbinic; if so here all the milk is forbidden, and especially for those who hold that when the stream is not interrupted it cooks the measure of peeling — then it is basar b'chalav d'oraisa.`,
    '8#_': `(seif 5 in hagahah) And it is good to be stringent where there is no great loss. And in lean meat one may say there are not two uncertainties — we are not expert between lean and fatty, and we are not expert between salting of roast and pot — peeling suffices; afterward I saw in Peri Megadim siman 105 in Siftei Da'at section 26 that he wrote plainly that even in lean meat, salting of roast is forbidden; and in my view it is not necessary.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#_': `["Except that rinsing is required, etc. And Bach wrote specifically when one of them is moist, but if both are dry, even rinsing is not required."]`,
    '2#א': `["To place meat, etc. And the same applies to place forbidden cold in a permitted cold vessel that is not customary to rinse — forbidden l'chatchila, because Rashba wrote in Tur HaShalem long and short 112b, and in the short version; and see siman 10 s.k. 20 and siman 69 s.k. 83."]`,
    '2#ב': `["And specifically, etc. The Mechaber's words require explanation, for his words in Terumat HaChacham and Darkei Moshe too require study, for in Terumat HaChacham general rule 17 din 2 he brought Orach Chaim's words (that R' Chananel wrote, brought by Beit Yosef s.k. 105) that gentile vessels rinsed well are permitted to place spices or onion or other sharp things that do not extract a hard dry thing in dry — end of his words; and afterward he wrote in din 4: however Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 91 in Ba'al HaItur's name wrote that everything requiring rinsing, such as cold meat in a forbidden cold bowl, is forbidden l'chatchila lest one eat without rinsing; and specifically cooked meat that is not customary to rinse, but what is customary to rinse such as raw meat and the like is permitted l'chatchila — end of his words; and it implies spices and the like that are not customary to rinse are forbidden to place in a forbidden vessel; yet one may distinguish that a forbidden vessel used hot is forbidden to use with permitted cold, and so Tur Yoreh De'ah explicitly in siman 121; however if forbidden cold use was used, permitted cold use is permitted even l'chatchila; therefore Rashba permitted salting meat in a yayin nesekh vessel even l'chatchila; and so with chametz vessels one may use matzah, dealing in such a case as in the Gemara, brought by Tur Orach Chaim siman 451; and so appears Yoreh De'ah siman 69; or distinguish whether the vessel was washed well initially — if washed well initially it is certainly permitted as Yoreh De'ah siman 122; and similarly Darkei Moshe who first challenged Ba'al HaItur from matzah and distinguished between moist permitted and dry, then wrote it further appears to distinguish between something that absorbed prohibition through hot or pickling and salting, etc., and this resolution appears primary to him; and from this understand his view that although the vessel is forbidden because of absorption of prohibition, nevertheless since prohibition was not absorbed through hot only through pickling or salting, permitted cold use is permitted (and Ba'er Hetev's words on this are confused — see there); this is the Mechaber's path, but all his words from beginning to end are unclear to me: at the outset what he wrote 'but one may distinguish,' etc., and so Taz in siman 121, etc. — I did not grasp his intent, for it is not found there as he says, and on the contrary it appears explicitly the opposite, as Tur wrote there: vessels used hot such as pots and kettles require hag'alah and immersion and are permitted; and Rabbenu Shemuel wrote specifically hag'alah then immersion, but if immersed first it is like immersing with a sheretz in hand; but R' Yitzchak wrote one may immerse first and if he only wants cold use he may immerse and use cold, and when he wants hot use he hag'alas and the prior immersion suffices — end of his words; and so Rosh end of Avodah Zarah; and what he wrote 'therefore Rashba permitted,' etc. — is irrelevant, for Rashba would permit anyway since raw meat is customary to rinse, and if because of salting it is nothing as Rashba wrote there for several reasons brought by Beit Yosef end of siman 105 — see there; and what he wrote 'and so with chametz,' etc. — is not agreed by all, but Rif in chapter Kol Shelah, Rambam, Ramban, and other poskim as explained Beit Yosef Orach Chaim, and Rashba in his novellae brought proof from Rif and Ramban; however Ra'ash chapter Kol Shelah and Tur there hold that with beit se'or and charoset one may use matzah cold (but they too infer from the Gemara only that except beit se'or and charoset one may use matzah hot, but cold they hold even beit se'or and charoset are permitted by reasoning — see there); and it implies for them too if chametz was absorbed hot, one may use matzah cold — what difference absorption by cold or hot; and on the contrary Ra'ash there brought the dispute whether for beit se'or and charoset even hag'alah does not help though for other vessels used hot hag'alah certainly helps for all; and in Avodah Zarah 34a they distinguish use hot and cold — for using permitted hot or pickled in them; but using permitted cold is obviously no distinction, as Ran chapter Kol Shelah, and so Semak, Shach, and Semag prohibition 141 (Noda BiYehudah 72b); and what he wrote 'and so appears Yoreh De'ah siman 69' — truly from Tur's words there it implies if one salted meat in an unperforated bowl, one may eat afterward cold without rinsing bowl or food; but the first view he brought is Rashba and supporters whom Beit Yosef brought that even if a forbidden boiling vessel was used, the law is so; if so the question returns, for Rashba ruled in Tur HaShalem long and short like Ba'al HaItur, and Beit Yosef brings him; and what he wrote 'and so appears Yoreh De'ah siman 122' — requires study; and he did not fulfill explaining distinguishing moist permitted from dry; for Rashba there explicitly permits eating cold moist in it — see there; and what Darkei Moshe distinguished between moist permitted and dry fails to explain, for Rashba explicitly permits cold moist there — see there; but in truth it appears to me what Orach Chaim wrote — gentile vessels rinsed well — means we know certainly they were rinsed well, such as a Jew rinsed now; and it does not mean rinsing specifically is required, for poskim who learned this hold thorough wiping is like rinsing everywhere; and Ba'al HaItur deals without wiping; or the main point is earthenware is different, forbidden even cold, and Taz follow their view that even earthenware permitted cold is permitted; and what Orach Chaim wrote that it does not extract hard dry in dry excludes sharp moist spices; but here, not extracting hard, even moist; however all occasional use, but regular use even cold forbidden lest use hot, as Rama hagahah siman 121 s.k. 5 — end of his words."]`,
    '3#_': `["If it touches it, it is forbidden to eat with cheese. Without rinsing, and we are concerned lest one forget and eat without rinsing; and it was explained in s.k. 1 that specifically when one of them is moist — see there."]`,
    '4#א': `["All is forbidden. If there is no 60, and specifically when falling into boiling meat in a pot; but not on roast hot outside the pot — per Tur and for us forbidden in every case up to 60, as siman 92 end s.k. 3 and siman 105 — see there."]`,
    '4#ב': `["Because the lower prevails. Meaning the lower that is hot heats the upper that is cold."]`,
    '4#ג': `["One peels the meat, etc. Because here the lower is cold it cools the upper, except it absorbed a little; therefore peeling is required; and see siman 105 s.k. 3 further regarding lower prevails — even hot milk on cold meat through pouring without interrupting stream, peeling suffices; and likewise if stream interrupted, peeling required — see there."]`,
    '4#ד': `["And in a place where the meat, etc. — requires study, for Tosafot Pesachim (96b) s.v. 'if there is,' etc. (brought by Beit Yosef s.k. 100, R' Yitzchak Netter section 13, Beit Yosef s.k. 105) and Hagahot Ashiri in Mahariach's name, R' Yitzchak, Mordechai, and Agudah there wrote that a gid of the sciatic nerve that was salted and cooked afterward requires 60 against the peel first forbidden through salting; and so Agur siman 1078 in Maharil's name on fowl cooked without removing veins — requires 60 against peel first forbidden through salting; and Beit Yosef brings it plainly siman 22 s.k. 4 and Bach there plainly siman 10 s.k. 5 regarding slaughtering with gentile knife — peel the place of slaughter, for even b'dieved forbidden the dish as any meat cooked without peeling — end of his words; and it appears the Mechaber is not challenged, for where peeling is required and cooked without peeling, permitted b'dieved — similar to here where milk is permitted because Tosafot, Rosh, and poskim wrote peeling does not apply, namely something blended; and similarly if meat cooked without peeling and blended unrecognizably, no peeling applies, permitted b'dieved; and now the general 'permitted b'dieved' is resolved — if recognizable, peeling would be required as siman 72; but if cooked and still recognizable where peeling applies, 60 required; and somewhat implied in Orach Chaim general rule 29 for the discerning — see there; and now the Mechaber's hagahah siman 98 s.k. 4 that a vessel forbidden through salting the measure of peeling requires afterward 60 is resolved; and therefore do not be lenient where recognizable and peeling applies; and anyway Maharai, Maharshal chapter 25 s.k. 75, and Terumat HaChacham ruled like Riva that even in milk 60 is required against the peel; and Terumat HaChacham general rule 23 s.k. 5 is stringent even in milk where there is not so much loss — see there."]`,
    '4#ה': `["And all the milk is permitted, etc. The reason — see the s.k. before this and see siman 69 s.k. 64."]`,
    '4#ו': `["And if they fell into each other while cold, etc. And Orach Chaim general rule 40 din 10 wrote in Mordechai and Semag's name — namely specifically in basar b'chalav; but if permitted cold fell into forbidden clear cold, peeling is required; and he brought the Mechaber at the beginning of general rule 22; and it requires study, for this is not found in Mordechai and Semag, and on the contrary their words imply the opposite — see there; and so appears in the Gemara chapter Keitzad Tzolin (Pesachim 76a) there is no distinction; and so Shach siman 20; and Maharil in Orach Chaim explicitly; and so implied by poskim."]`,
    '5#א': `["That is, etc. The Mechaber holds like Ran whom Beit Yosef ruled, namely that after waiting the measure of salting for a pot it is hot, and not beforehand; and that which is explained in his words siman 69 s.k. 20 that even beforehand it is hot, and somewhat implies there that after waiting the measure of salting it is not hot — it must be different there, for it deals with blood prohibition, and therefore blood and salt are hot at salting (and as Mechaber wrote in hagahah that even where meat is not considered hot, its gravy is considered hot — see there), but not when it waited the measure of salting when the strength of salt ceased because it expelled all blood; but here it deals with meat and cheese prohibition — as long as not salted properly the meat is not hot, but when it waited the measure of salting it is considered hot, for the strength of salt did not cease since it expelled nothing; certainly it deals that meat was already salted and rinsed and now salted again, for otherwise cheese would be forbidden because of blood; and the Mechaber in hagahah compared meat and cheese to blood prohibition — and because the Mechaber followed Terumat HaDeshen siman 159, I must respond to his words as I will explain in s.k. 12; therefore necessity compels bringing Terumat HaDeshen briefly and responding per my humble opinion: in Terumat HaDeshen they asked regarding a brit milah meal where much meat was salted in one vessel and geese in another, after waiting the measure of salting and more they took and mixed all meat with geese, turned everything top to bottom until all touched, and afterward one goose was found treifah and there is no 60 even against one goose; he answered to permit with great loss and mitzvah meal, for Tosafot and Rosh wrote in chapter 25 for one version that after the measure of salting the strength of salt ceased since it expelled all blood; and so Semag (prohibition 137 beginning 41) — reasonable reason; but in Semak, Shach, and Hagahot Ashiri from Avodah Zarah wrote that salted meat and cheese touching each other are forbidden even after the measure of salting; and one may strain to distinguish only the salt itself weakened but the piece itself remains hot forever — if so what is Rosh and Semag's challenge from the dove in brine that if brine is properly salted the dove is forbidden though brine was salted days ago; and one may answer it is different there because salt did not cease and expelled nothing; nevertheless without challenge, those geonim clearly disagree; except Semag itself also wrote like Semak and supporters that below it is forbidden; therefore it appears not to be lenient except with mitzvah meal and great loss; and in short Maharshal chapter 25 siman 68 challenged and resolved his challenge, following him Bach siman 69 — their words are strained as the examiner will see; and in any case no reason to distinguish if salt is not hot why the piece is hot; but per my humble opinion from poskim' implication we do not say the strength of salt ceased except where it expelled blood, for salt's way is to expel blood, not other things; and as Rosh and Tur siman 90 wrote — thus only in that case after waiting the measure of salting the strength of salt ceased since it expelled blood; unlike other things, and therefore Semak and supporters dealing with meat and cheese from basar b'chalav, not blood, and the strength of salt did not cease since it expelled nothing — obviously after the measure of salting it is considered hot; and so Orach Chaim general rule 58 din 32 — kosher meat the salt always in its strength, for it did not expel anything — see there; and this is what Rosh and Semag wrote that in brine the strength of salt did not cease since it expelled nothing — this appears clearly to me; if so the geonim do not disagree, Semag's words are resolved, and Tur and Mechaber's words agreeing; and I proposed this to the Gaon our master and teacher and other great teachers and they discussed and agreed; and therefore it appeared to permit even without great loss and mitzvah meal; but since from other poskim regarding Rashi's incident it implies even regarding blood we do not say the strength of salt ceased after the measure of salting — therefore do not add — not to permit except with great loss and mitzvah meal; and therefore Mechaber wrote in Semak hagahah that Riva says gravy exuded from meat after the measure of salting has no law of hot blood to forbid cheeses where it fell (brought by Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe siman 69) — meaning even after the measure of salting it is considered hot, only it has no law of blood since it waited the measure and all its blood left; and so in Hagahot Shach section 14; and therefore do not add — permit only with great loss and mitzvah meal; and nevertheless within the measure of salting always forbidden even with great loss and mitzvah meal, for all poskim hold within the measure it is hot, and Ran is singular against them; and Rashba in Mishmeret HaBayit (73) refuted this twice — see there."]`,
    '5#ב': `["And there is no difference, etc. And above siman 70 s.k. 4 and below siman 105 s.k. 11 he ruled like Rashba to distinguish in salted meat between upper and lower — it must be specifically to forbid all of it, namely in fatty meat there is a difference, but to forbid the measure of peeling such as lean meat there is no difference, for although lower prevails, nevertheless peeling is required as seif 4; and so implied from Rashba in Tur HaShalem short and Shulchan Aruch there (and investigate Beit Yosef this siman s.v. 'and it does not depend,' etc. — it does not appear so)."]`,
    '5#ג': `["Therefore kosher meat and cheese, etc. And it is not relevant here to say since busy expelling it does not absorb, or as in other places that specifically regarding blood we say this and not other prohibitions, as poskim wrote in many places, not like Orach Chaim general rule 12 din 11 — see there."]`,
    '5#ד': `["Meaning the impure is dry, etc. And in Hagahot Shach that Beit Yosef brought siman 70 — it implies soft milk not congealed is not called a clear substance; and so Hagahot Ashiri chapter 25 from Avodah Zarah, and so Maharshal chapter 25 section 80."]`,
    '5#ה': `["The pure is forbidden since it is not salted because of its salt, etc. — so it should read; and for hot meat falling into cold milk only the measure of peeling is forbidden, and we do not say because of its heat it absorbs the clear prohibition next to it — there since lower prevails the meat cooled, but in salting lower prevails does not apply."]`,
    '5#ו': `["And both are forbidden. Meaning in basar b'chalav even if only meat was salted, both are forbidden; but if only milk was salted, Terumat HaChacham general rule 22 section 2 implies from Mordechai that milk is permitted, unlike Semag; and he ruled so there; and it is astonishing, for Mordechai chapter 25 (section 204) says the opposite like Semag; and it appears a scribal error — it should read Hagahot Mordechai; and Mordechai in Hagahot Mordechai in Maharaf's name, also Orach Chaim general rule 40 din 14; and nevertheless requires study for practical law, since Mordechai and Semag forbid — who will tell us to be lenient; also Maharshal in Orach Chaim wrote, etc., and it requires study, for in Semag it is forbidden; and in Hagahot Shach that Beit Yosef brought siman 70 it implies soft milk not congealed is not a clear substance, and so Maharshal."]`,
    '5#ז': `["But if it is completely dry, etc. It implies even if salted, all is permitted; and so Orach Chaim wrote there."]`,
    '5#ח': `["And if it fell only on one place, etc. Meaning if it fell on the entire vessel peeling does not apply, for the vessel would be ruined or great effort; and so Terumat HaChacham general rule 13 and Orach Chaim general rule 40 din 12; and therefore if no loss and one wishes to exert himself, it is permitted; and although below siman 121 s.k. 2 and Orach Chaim siman 451 s.k. 16 Mechaber wrote in hagahah that wherever hag'alah is required, peeling the vessel does not help — there it deals with something absorbed through hot and absorbed in the entire vessel; but salted gravy is absorbed only the measure of peeling, therefore peeling suffices; and see siman 69 s.k. 16 and 17 and what is written there and below siman 121 and what is written there."]`,
    '6#א': `["If it were fatty, etc. Meaning if one of the pieces were fatty — whether meat or cheese — all is forbidden, for then one goes and flavors the other and it is as if all is fatty, as above siman 70 s.k. 4 and siman 105."]`,
    '6#ב': `["And see below siman 105 s.k. 9. It is explained that for us there is no difference between lean and fatty, and all salting we estimate at sixty — see there."]`,
    '7#א': `["But hot roast, etc. Know there are three views: Sefer HaTerumah, Sha'arei Dura, Rosh, Tur, Maharil, and other poskim hold that hot roast, even cold, falling into milk, even if eaten because of its salt, requires peeling; and if the meat has cracks or is seasoned with spices, even if live, all is forbidden; and Ra'avad, Rashba, and Ran hold if roast is cold, only rinsing is never required except peeling if hot; and if cracks or seasoned and hot, all forbidden; and from Rashba's words there it appears so is Rashi's view, and so truly appears Rashi's view in chapter Keitzad Tzolin (Pesachim 76) end of 76a — see there; and Maharshal chapter 25 siman 69 did not look except Rashi chapter 25, therefore wrote there is no implication from Rashi; and Avodah Zarah, Hagahot Mordechai in Maharaf's name, Semak section 205 (96b), appear roast cold requires peeling, and if seasoned or cracks and cold roast, all forbidden, but not live; and so Semag — see there prohibitions 137 page 47 side 4 and 138 page 49 side 3; and so Rosh apparently, and Maharam of Rottenburg responsum 218, and so all in siman 106 (beginning page 123); and so appears Orach Chaim general rule 40; and so Maharai, and Maharshal there; and Mechaber in Terumat HaChacham general rule 22 only here in hagahah is lenient with great loss — and it requires study whether to be lenient since many poskim hold so even for live; and furthermore in the Gemara it appears to deal with cold roast, as Tosafot wrote chapter Keitzad Tzolin 75b s.v. 'and if roast,' etc., and Pesachim 96b s.v. 'until it reaches,' etc. — see there; and so Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 15 from Rambam; and Ba'er Hetev ruled plainly like Mechaber and it is not clear at all."]`,
    '7#ב': `["Even if eaten because of its salt, etc. It is imprecise — he should have taught us even if not salted at all, since it is hot like any hot into cold requiring peeling; and it should read: because since it is eaten because of its salt and was not salted at all, one law for both — he did not go into fine distinction."]`,
    '7#ג': `["Requires peeling. Like any hot into cold requiring peeling; and do not challenge how peeling suffices though milk is soft and the roast that fell seems cooked in it; and one may answer since it fell into milk it is hot into cold, and although roast sinks into milk and milk floats on roast, it is not cold into hot — whatever is in its place prevails and cools the roast that fell; therefore peeling suffices — end of Rashba's words; and he wrote in section 38 page 234a: and per what we rule below siman 105 s.k. 11 that in salting we do not say upper/lower distinction, initially it is not a question; and it is astonishing — per his words the question stands more, for it is as if cooked and one cannot answer it is hot into cold; and certainly Rashba did not come from salting, for it was eaten because of its salt, but from hot roast — as his words make plain."]`,
    '7#ד': `["And likewise baked and cooked. Meaning baked and cooked are considered like roast — one says hot, one says cold."]`,
    '7#ה': `["And some say even if they are cold the law is thus. Because since roasted, baked, or cooked, although cold it is soft and absorbs; and from poskim' words it implies no difference between slightly salted eaten because of salt and never salted — forever in cold roast the law is thus; and so Maharshal concludes in his book there. Written in Hagahot Maimoniyot there: nevertheless milk is permitted and we do not say roast, baked, or cooked expels; and he brings it in Orach Chaim and Terumat HaChacham there; and the law if milk was salted — see s.k. 15."]`,
    '7#ו': `["And some say even if cold, etc. From this language it implies the Mechaber agrees here too regarding hot roast the law is thus — if not seasoned and no cracks, peeling suffices — and so is primary, since Ra'avad, Rashi, Rashba, and Ran hold so regarding hot roast, though Hagahot Maimoniyot and Orach Chaim there hold hot roast does not suffice with peeling — see there; however all this specifically when hot roast fell into milk; but if milk or other prohibition fell on hot roast — its law in siman 92 s.k. 3 and siman 105 — see there."]`,
    '8#_': `["There is no basar b'chalav, etc. — see introduction siman 87 and what is written there."]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '2#א': `Everything that requires rinsing, etc. Tur wrote in this regard "everything," etc. — one may challenge: what does "everything" mean, for explicitly in the Mishnah we say "provided they do not touch each other"; and one may answer that specifically meat and cheese themselves, but regarding their vessels one need not be careful — it teaches that everything requiring rinsing, even in vessels, are equal; and one may challenge further: Beit Yosef wrote in siman 2 that wherever b'dieved is permitted we are not concerned initially lest one forget, as will be cited in seif 4; if so why are we concerned here lest one eat without rinsing, since if one eats without rinsing there is no prohibition, for even where peeling is required some hold it is permitted b'dieved without peeling? And one may answer that where there is no clear prohibition before us but only doubt requiring investigation afterward, since b'dieved it is permitted one may rely l'chatchila on the investigation afterward; unlike here where there is clear prohibition in placing kosher meat in a forbidden vessel, only that it has remedy through rinsing afterward — we do not rely on rinsing afterward lest one forget.`,
    '2#ב': `Forbidden l'chatchila, etc. Maharshal chapter 25 siman 44 wrote, and therefore travelers who eat salted herring on gentile vessels do not act well, for rinsing is required after placing on them; and all the more those who buy salted cucumbers called ogrekes with brine and eat in their bowl, for even the cucumbers require rinsing if placed in their bowl; if so the brine, to which rinsing does not apply, is all forbidden — end of his words; and Terumat HaChacham general rule 16 wrote that in time of pressure, such as lodging in a gentile house, it is considered b'dieved — end of his words.`,
    '2#ג': `And specifically in a thing that has moisture, etc. Darkei Moshe proved this from the law of a vessel used for chametz in cold use that one may use matzah, as in Orach Chaim siman 451; and further he wrote there and Terumat HaChacham general rule 17 on what Tur wrote here that anything not customary to rinse is forbidden in a forbidden cold bowl — he challenged this from Orach Chaim that gentile vessels rinsed well are permitted to place dry spices, etc.; and he answered — see there; and from all this it is explained that a vessel used for forbidden cold use is permitted to place dry hot food, as we noted regarding chametz vessels; if so a fortiori permitted cold moist use is permitted, more than dry hot is less than cold moist, as Orach Chaim siman 451 and the Rashba case of salting in a yayin nesekh vessel; and in this hagahah it implies that even for dry use we require that initial use was cold, and then cold use is permitted specifically; and this is very astonishing, for it was already explained that it is not so except per the language of this hagahah — it is astonishing that he wrote it is permitted to place dry food, etc. — this is superfluous language already mentioned initially; and it appears there is a scribal error and it should read: but a completely dry thing, or if the vessel did not absorb only through cold use, it is permitted to place moist food without rinsing; and the explanation is that he refers to Shulchan Aruch's forbidden cold bowl where hot use was used, and on this he said dry use is permitted without rinsing, and if forbidden use was not used initially only cold, afterward permitted without rinsing even for moist food — as appears to me, and Levush also mentioned only dry alone, per my view.`,
    '4#א': `That mixed together. And if in a vessel — see siman 105 seif 3 and the measure of heat — how much is it — also in siman 105.`,
    '4#ב': `All is forbidden. Because the lower prevails — and this is specifically regarding prohibition because of itself; but if it is absorbed prohibition it does not pass from piece to piece without gravy — so Terumat HaChacham general rule 23; and see siman 105 s.k. 7 what is written there.`,
    '4#ג': `One peels the meat. In the Gemara we say the reason is that it absorbed a little; Rashi explained it cooled it; and in Orach Chaim general rule 29 he wrote it weighed upon it; and so in Mordechai chapter 25.`,
    '4#ד': `And all the milk is permitted. Tur brought Riva's view that there must be 60 in the milk against the peel forbidden from it; and Maharai wrote in Hagahot Shach that although elsewhere a peel does not forbid its fellow even if the peels are fatty, etc., because there is no power for what is forbidden where the prohibition cannot go — likewise there is no meat taste in all the milk; nevertheless 60 is required since it is a moist thing and mixes together; and it appears practice is stringent, not like Rosh — end of his words; and Shulchan Aruch ruled like Rosh, namely R' Yitzchak and Rambam who do not require 60 against the peel; and Orach Chaim general rule 59 rules leniently like Rosh in the name of Maharam, that wherever the Sages required peeling and it was cooked thus without peeling, even without 60 against the peel it is permitted even l'chatchila, for the Sages required peeling only as an initial stringency — end of his words; and in general rule 36 he wrote the opposite, that a bowl forbidden the measure of peeling forbids up to 60 against the peel; and Terumat HaDeshen wrote his words in general rule 36 are per Riva's view, and it is a great strain that Riva's view was not mentioned there, and on the contrary his words are to rule plainly; and I wonder at Maharai in the name of Rosh to be lenient, for Rosh brought two views in chapter 25 and did not decide, and on the contrary Maharshal wrote therefore he brought Riva's view last, for so is his conclusion; and in my humble opinion it can be proven from Rosh's words in chapter 7 of Chullin the opposite, and Tur brought it in siman 10 regarding slaughtering with a gentile knife — peel the place of slaughter; and Rosh wrote one should not slaughter l'chatchila relying that he will peel afterward — it turns out that whatever is not peeled he eats prohibition even b'dieved, for whatever is permitted b'dieved we are not concerned initially lest one forget — and this is proven in siman 1 in Tur regarding "however l'chatchila they may give him to slaughter relying that he will inspect," and siman 2 that we are concerned lest he forget and not inspect, etc.; and so Beit Yosef there explicitly; however this can be rejected — perhaps permitted b'dieved without peeling, nevertheless one may not rely l'chatchila since there is clear prohibition with a remedy, as the beginning of this siman and Maharshal chapter 25 siman 60 ruled stringently like Riva that 60 is required against the peel even b'dieved; and he further wrote that even if there is 60 in the meat against the milk, nevertheless the meat requires peeling, for it did not absorb throughout; also in investigation he brought Maharshal's view to be stringent; and Rama ruled here and in Terumat HaChacham that milk does not require 60 against the peel — thus he does not hold like Riva; and so Terumat HaChacham general rule 23 — if one poured permitted food into a forbidden bowl it is permitted, for by law only peeling is required; and it is astonishing that in siman 69 seif 16 he ruled that for a moist hot thing 60 is required against the peel — they are Rashba's words there in Beit Yosef, and so Terumat HaChacham general rule 12 — namely like Riva; thus his words contradict each other; and since Rashba also holds so, we should rule like Riva to be stringent, and everywhere 60 is required against the peel; and so in Mordechai chapter Kol HaBasar in an incident before Rabbenu Yehudah — they kneaded dough, etc., he ruled there 60 is required against the peel of the bowl; and so implied at the end of seif 5 in hagahah regarding gravy falling on a vessel — hag'alah and earthenware breaking are required, and for wooden vessels peeling suffices — thus b'dieved we require nullification of what was forbidden the measure of peeling; and further proof from what Rosh brought, Beit Yosef beginning siman 96 — a slice of cress cut with a forbidden knife is all forbidden though it only forbids the measure of peeling, because the slices are small and one cannot know where it was cut, therefore all was forbidden — end of his words, from which we learn that where peeling is impossible, all is forbidden; and further proof from Orach Chaim siman 95 seif 3 — if one poured from a kli rishon of meat onto a milk vessel it forbids all of it, etc. — end of his words; and although pouring does not forbid more than the measure of peeling, nevertheless it forbids all where peeling is impossible; and further proof from Rama siman 98 seif 4 — but if through hot salting, etc., one estimates only the measure of peeling, etc. — end of his words; and further proof from Orach Chaim general rule 36 din 6 — if placed in a boiling pot, 60 is required against its peel, etc.; and I saw in hagahah at the end of Orach Chaim in the name of Maharai that practice is like Riva to require sixty, and so is practical law.`,
    '5#א': `And for great need, etc. — above siman 69 s.k. 20 we explained these matters.`,
    '5#ב': `Therefore salted meat and cheese. It should read moist.`,
    '5#ג': `Must peel both at the place of contact. And we do not say as it absorbs so it expels, only regarding blood — so Mordechai chapter 25.`,
    '5#ד': `Clear substance — the pure is forbidden since it is not eaten because of its salt, it absorbs the prohibition next to it, etc. — so it should read.`,
    '7#_': `And some say even if they are cold. This is Rosh's view, who explained so the incident of the dove that fell into milk in the Gemara; and Maharai wrote in Hagahot Shach, etc. — in Hagahot Ashiri from Avodah Zarah he wrote that live meat has no cracks; and so is our practice that before salting the meat we soak it and there is much blood mixed with water and we are not precise whether there are cracks in the meat that would forbid from blood and water together; and sometimes it soaks a long time and one may distinguish between blood and other prohibitions; and Maharshal chapter 25 siman 69 wrote on this — what appeared to him from Maharai's words regarding roast that even if roast and milk were not salted at all one should forbid, for he did not bring proof to permit live meat except from soaking meat, etc. — it does not appear to me, for cold does not absorb from cold even if seasoned, and the Sages only forbade in the dove that fell into brine that was slightly salted — then forbidden if it has cracks; but if not salted at all, neither meat nor brine — no prohibition at all in cold, even roast; but I fear to disagree with Semak, Semag, Hagahot Maimoniyot, etc., who wrote plainly that the law is so even if neither meat nor milk was salted, etc. — I fear to disagree with them l'chatchila and to permit what they forbid — end of his words; it implies that in live meat it is obviously permitted if neither was salted; and in roast, if one was salted even if eaten because of its salt, it is forbidden even per Maharshal, for at least one was slightly salted; and Rama who wrote that with great loss one may be lenient per the first view lenient regarding eaten because of its salt — all that is not hot roast; and above in seif 5 he wrote we are not expert and should forbid even salting roast without great loss; and it appears to me we do not combine two leniencies for great loss; however in hot roast without seasoning and without cracks, peeling suffices — and so appears to me; however all this when hot roast fell into milk, but if milk or other prohibition fell on hot roast — its law in siman 92 and 105.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(siman 91 seif 2) Everything that requires rinsing, such as placing permitted cold meat in a forbidden cold bowl. In my humble opinion the source of this law is the Gemara chapter Kol HaBasar (daf 111) where we say a knife with which one slaughtered is forbidden to eat with hot use; cold — they say — requires rinsing; and Tosafot, Rosh, and Shach in chapter 1 of Chullin (daf 8) explained that "requires rinsing" refers to the knife; and so too Ran in chapter Kol Shelah regarding all vessels — see there; and with this one resolves what Ran wrote in the margin that requires study — see there and investigate. But through rinsing the knife it is permitted to cut cold meat and one need not rinse the permitted food afterward; and so Taz siman 10 s.k. 2 and siman 69 seif 16 — see there. Therefore Taz here also deals thus when the bowl was not rinsed; for if after rinsing the bowl it is permitted to place cold permitted meat without rinsing the meat — so too Shach; and this is clear from the language of the hagahah that wrote it is permitted to place dry food without rinsing at all — meaning it deals without rinsing the vessel; and we learn in the Gemara there (daf 111) that Rav Ami salted meat in that earthenware pan and broke it; and from breaking we learn that an earthenware vessel used for forbidden hot use is forbidden to use even cold, even through rinsing the vessel; and so explicitly in Me'iri general rule 11 s.k. 6 — see there; because their power of absorption is great and they expel even in cold use, therefore all cold use requires rinsing. And therefore it is forbidden l'chatchila lest one forget to rinse. And see Taz siman 69; and this is the view of most poskim — Rif and Ramban chapter Kol Shelah per their text there, and Rashba in his novellae brought by Beit Yosef siman 69, and Ran chapter 25, and Maggid Mishneh chapter 6 of Forbidden Foods, and so it appears to me this is Ra'avad's view and Kol Bo, and Beit Yosef brings them in Darkei Moshe siman 121 regarding green earthenware bowls — forbidden to use hot forever; and likewise any moist thing even in cold use — and so Shulchan Aruch siman 122; except he called it "forbidden cold use" and did not mention earthenware, and I do not know why. And it appears Beit Yosef copied Ra'avad from Orach Chaim and there earthenware is not mentioned — perhaps it deals with other vessels; but Darkei Moshe copied Ra'avad from Kol Bo and there it is explicit as I wrote; and so Orach Chaim and Acharonim siman 121 s.k. 7 — that earthenware one should be concerned perhaps the gentile used it hot and requires breaking. And regarding salted food I wrote in siman 69 that it is unclear that there is no salting for vessels; nevertheless since in any case they expel even in cold use — why must one rinse the cold use? And if so salt, which is like hot regarding meat, absorbs in it the exudation of prohibition; and it is literally like what Ran wrote in chapter Kol HaBasar and Shach siman 10 s.k. 19 regarding a knife that slaughtered a treifah — the knife itself is not forbidden, only requires rinsing, and if one slaughtered kosher without rinsing, peeling is required; and so in Piskei HaRosh section 90. However in Me'iri general rule 22 s.k. 15 it writes specifically regarding a clear substance — investigate; and in Orach Chaim general rule 58 it implies that if it cannot be rinsed it is permitted like peeling. Know that Rav Ami's pan that broke — why salt live meat already salted and rinsed, as hagahah siman 69 seif 16? And peeling does not apply here, for live meat is customary to rinse as Taz wrote here in this seif; rather we learn that in earthenware there is no remedy except peeling as written there siman 69. And I saw the Mechaber in Darkei Moshe siman 121 wants to say that it is permitted to use permitted cold in a forbidden vessel even earthenware, and it implies there from his words even cold moist use; and it is not clear, and all our building there from Tur's words is destroyed — for what he analyzes from Tur regarding a vessel bought from a gentile — immerse and use cold — is nothing, for Tur does not deal with earthenware but metal vessels since he mentions immersion; and what Darkei Moshe wrote, and Tur siman 94 — there is no proof that Tur deals only with completely dry things since he mentions fruits; and so in Tosafot chapter Ein Ma'amidin and Pit'hei Teshuva siman 56 and Mordechai in the name of Rabbenu Tam and Beit Yosef and Be'er HaGolah and as written there s.k. 5 — in completely dry things rinsing is not required even in earthenware; and this is the hagahah here: it is permitted to place dry food without rinsing at all — meaning even in earthenware; investigate that Darkei Moshe contradicts himself, for he wrote regarding a gentile earthenware vessel that breaking is required — investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(there seif 4) Hot meat and milk that mixed together. This deals with a kli rishon; and as hagahah siman 105 s.k. 3; and if so even for benefit all is forbidden, for kli rishon is cooking and forbidden d'oraisa; and the same for cold into hot; but not hot into cold, for that is not the manner of cooking as siman 87; and so I found in Ran chapter Kol HaBasar, unlike Levush there and Me'iri general rule 8; and so in responsum Makom Shmuel that I brought siman 101.`,
    '3#_': `(there) And all the milk is permitted. This is Rabbenu Tam's view; but Riva's view requires 60 in the milk against the peel forbidden from it; and it is difficult to estimate 60 in milk against its peel; and it appears we estimate that there should be 60 in the milk against the peel of the meat that fell into it, for more than the measure of peeling does not give taste in milk; and it is literally like hagahah above siman 69 s.k. 16 regarding an unperforated vessel — see there and investigate; and in siman 87 I wrote that it is proven from Pesachim per Rabbenu Tam that where Rav holds the upper prevails, peeling suffices where the lower is hot; and Rav should be challenged — why is the milk permitted, it is nevelah milk forbidden the measure of peeling and forbids everything, for min b'mino is not nullified — he holds; and as we challenge Rav in chapter Kol HaBasar (daf 108); but we learn where peeling is impossible it is permitted b'dieved; and with this Tosafot's challenge in Pesachim (daf 75) s.v. "he takes," etc., is resolved well per Rabbenu Tam's view — see hagahah siman 105; and Rambam chapter 8 of Forbidden Foods appears like Riva as I wrote in s.k. 4, unlike Tur who wrote all the milk is permitted; and it appears from Rambam's words, and it is not so, for Rambam relied on what is written chapter 8 of Forbidden Foods.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `And even in a place where peeling is required, etc. — in my humble opinion it is not comparable, for there the permitted b'dieved without peeling is when it was cooked and peeling is impossible afterward, for the absorbed prohibition goes out through peeling, but it already became nevelah; and on this we say b'dieved it is permitted; unlike here where certainly if one does not do the remedy of rinsing and eats it, he eats a prohibition, and this is not a b'dieved permission at all; therefore it is not comparable to siman 2 which is permitted b'dieved.`,
    '2#_': `Forbidden l'chatchila lest one eat. Avodat HaTaharah; and see in responsum Beit Avraham siman 29 — thick milk or butter that was hot and cooled, but nevertheless it was soft and dissolved at the time of falling on the meat and congealed there on the meat's surface — plain rinsing does not help and thorough rinsing with great rubbing is required until one knows by estimate that the prohibition left him; and I brought there Rashba's novellae and what is written there; and see Chavat Da'at who wrote that cutting meat with a knife that cut cheese has "pressure" and greasiness and requires rubbing, etc.; and from R' Alexander Ziskind in Terumat HaDeshen siman 10 s.k. 17 it is clear that where there is pressure, insertion is required; and he brought there Orach Chaim general rule 58 — see there at length for several distinctions in this.`,
    '3#_': `That he wrote and it appears to them that if chametz was absorbed hot, etc. — one may say it appears to them thus, for otherwise whence would we learn that it is permitted to use matzah hot — perhaps it speaks of cold use; and it is strained to say they imply that even for beit se'or and charoset it is permitted cold, and since it says except beit se'or and charoset it proves it deals with hot use; but one may say that in truth beit se'or and charoset are forbidden even cold, but if we say that even if chametz was absorbed hot it is permitted cold — what difference whether absorbed by hot or cold — therefore when it says permitted to use matzah it means even hot use; and therefore it specifically mentions chametz was used cold; and nevertheless per Rosh's words one must explain what Tur wrote below siman 94, etc., that it implies even cold moist use, and so Beit Yosef there, and it implies even something not customary to rinse like fruits, etc.; and therefore what is written "and it implies even something not customary," etc. — is only for permission, for certainly if one will not forget he will rinse it, unlike cold moist where rinsing does not apply.`,
    '4#_': `And with this Tur below siman 94, etc., is resolved — that it implies even cold moist use, and so Beit Yosef there, and it implies even something not customary to rinse like fruits, etc.; and therefore what is written is only for permission.`,
    '5#_': `Because the lower prevails. Avodat HaTaharah; and see in responsum Noda BiYehudah (?) siman 28 — they disagreed whether the lower or upper prevails; they challenged him how this dispute applies since we taste for k'feila; and he answered to resolve that from sensation it is impossible to stand on the test, for even on a kli rishon on fire it is not certain that it expels — sometimes it does not expel — but we are stringent and say it expels; therefore when there is no k'feila or min b'mino where one cannot determine whether it absorbed or not — they disagreed; one holds the lower prevails and one the upper; and likewise in what they disagreed in Chullin in all prohibitions whether we estimate at 60 or 100 — the Sages did not decide the matter by tasting — per Tosafot's view there that it does not give taste there is no prohibition — see there; written in responsum Admat Kodesh part 2 Yoreh De'ah siman 11 seif 5 — where salt is found only in a gentile's shop and it was reported that he buys salt in which kosher and non-kosher and treifah animals were salted — forbidden to salt in it and forbidden to put it in a cooked dish; if placed, it is nullified in 60, for the prohibition is not because of itself but because of blood in it — see there; also asked about salt left in the bottom of a vessel used to salt fish — if one leaves the salt until it dries in heat, may one salt meat in it — he answered forbidden, not because of danger, for there is no danger in salting as explained siman 70, but because blood of fish was already absorbed in it and forbidden to salt again; investigate per what he wrote above that salt in which meat was salted was permitted to salt again because the strength of the salt did not cease, only the prohibition is blood absorbed in it — if so fish blood which is permitted should be permitted to salt meat afterward; perhaps it deals with impure fish; investigate for practical law there is reason to forbid; written in responsum Admat Kodesh part 2 Yoreh De'ah siman 1 — grape clusters in which small worms called fraks are found — forbidden to eat because due to their thinness they are not recognizable, and even inspection and cooking do not help, for they checked and saw they did not melt in cooking and remained whole.`,
  },
};
